# dbelo — the one cron entry this server needs.
#
# Install it for the deploy user (NOT root):
#
#     crontab -u www-data -e
#
# and paste the line below. Do NOT run `crontab -u www-data deploy/crontab`
# unless you mean it: that REPLACES the user's whole crontab rather than
# adding to it, and anything else already scheduled there disappears.
#
# ── WHY ONLY ONE LINE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Laravel does not want a cron entry per task. Every scheduled job in the
# application is declared in routes/console.php, and this single line wakes
# the scheduler once a minute so it can decide which of them are due. Adding
# a second cron for anything already declared there means it runs twice.
#
# ── WHAT STOPS WORKING WITHOUT IT ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing fails loudly. That is the problem with forgetting it:
#
#   RollupStatsCommand      hourly    the admin dashboard chart stops
#                                     advancing and quietly shows stale days
#   QueueHeartbeat          5 min     the panel loses its proof that a worker
#                                     is alive — see below, this one is worse
#                                     than it sounds
#   model:prune             daily     the activity log and error groups grow
#                                     without limit
#   the newsletter digest   weekly    subscribers simply never hear from you
#
# The heartbeat is the one to care about. App\Services\QueueHealth decides
# whether the queue is healthy from two signals: when a worker was last seen,
# and how old the oldest unclaimed job is. The heartbeat feeds both — it
# stamps the cache when it runs, and when no worker is running the heartbeats
# themselves pile up in the jobs table and give the stall alarm something to
# measure even on a quiet day when nobody uploaded anything.
#
# So: no cron → no heartbeat → a dead queue worker looks exactly like a
# working one until somebody uploads a sound and waits ten minutes.
#
# ── THE LINE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Output goes to /dev/null on purpose. The scheduler runs 1,440 times a day
# and mails its output to the local user by default, which fills a mailbox
# nobody reads with nothing. Anything worth knowing is already in
# storage/logs and in the panel.

* * * * * cd /var/www/dbelo && php artisan schedule:run >> /dev/null 2>&1

# ── VERIFY IT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# After installing, wait one minute and run:
#
#     php artisan schedule:list
#
# It prints every task and the next time each is due. If a task you expect
# is missing, it is not the cron — it is routes/console.php.
#
# To confirm the cron itself is firing, watch the heartbeat: the Queue screen
# in the panel shows when a worker was last seen. Within five minutes of
# installing this it should stop saying "unknown".
